    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "depth": 0,
      "kind": "section",
      "number": "2",
      "title": "Definition",
      "body": "Klappvisor Bascinet is a medieval helmet that originated in the 14th century.\n\"Klappvisor\" is German and translates to \"folding visor.\" The single brow-mounted hinge is a distinctive feature of early bascinet visor designs.\n\nThese bascinet visors were often low profile to fit underneath a great helm, but would also serve as a primary defense on their own.\n\nKlappvisor uses a solid metal visor to protect the face, attached on the forehead by a piece of metal, the “spoon”.\nKlappvisor was used from 1340 to 1400, mainly in Germany and Italy.\n\nKlappvisor is Approved for 14th century and Transitional armors.\n\nKlappvisor is Denied for 15th century armors.\n\nSpoon",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
